+# HIP (ROCm / AMDGPU) Support for Halide
+
+Halide can target AMD GPUs natively through the HIP runtime: device kernels
+are compiled by LLVM's AMDGPU backend into an HSA code object (`.hsaco`) as
+part of Halide code generation, and the Halide runtime loads and launches them
+through `libamdhip64` at execution time. Both JIT and AOT usage are supported
+via the `hip` target flag plus an architecture feature (e.g.
+`HL_JIT_TARGET=host-hip-hip_gfx942`).
+
+The HIP backend is new and should be considered **EXPERIMENTAL**. At the time
+of writing it has been validated only structurally (the produced code objects
+have been checked with `llvm-readelf` / `llvm-objdump`); **it has not yet been
+run on AMD hardware**. See [Current Status](#current-status) for exactly what
+has and has not been verified.
+
+AMD GPUs were already reachable from Halide through the OpenCL and Vulkan
+backends. The HIP backend is the first path that emits native AMDGPU machine
+code from Halide's own LLVM pipeline, which allows architecture-specific
+codegen (wave size, LDS, native atomics, native `f16`) and removes the
+OpenCL/SPIR-V translation layer.
+
+## Compiling Halide w/HIP Support
+
+Halide needs an LLVM (version 21 or newer, matching Halide's normal LLVM
+requirement) that was built with:
+
+- the `AMDGPU` target, and
+- `lld` (the ELF linker library `lldELF` + `lldCommon`, plus its CMake package).
+
+The `AMDGPU` component is optional in Halide's CMake configuration; it is
+enabled automatically when the LLVM you point Halide at provides it. If the
+LLVM has `AMDGPU` but no `lld`, the HIP backend is disabled with a status
+message (the same policy as the WebAssembly backend). You can check with:
+
+```
+% llvm-config --targets-built | tr ' ' '\n' | grep AMDGPU
+% ls $LLVM_ROOT/lib/cmake/lld/LLDConfig.cmake
+```
+
+The official LLVM release tarballs (e.g. `LLVM-22.1.8-Linux-X64.tar.xz` from
+https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/releases) contain both. Distribution
+packages from https://apt.llvm.org/ (`llvm-22-dev`, `liblld-22-dev`) also work.
+
+Configure and build as usual, pointing at that LLVM:
+
+```
+% cmake -G Ninja -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
+      -DLLVM_DIR=$LLVM_ROOT/lib/cmake/llvm \
+      -DClang_DIR=$LLVM_ROOT/lib/cmake/clang \
+      -DLLD_DIR=$LLVM_ROOT/lib/cmake/lld
+% cmake --build build --config Release
+```
+
+Look for `AMDGPU` in the list of LLVM components CMake reports; the library is
+then built with `WITH_AMDGPU` defined.
+
+Notes:
+
+- **ROCm is not required to build Halide.** Code generation only needs LLVM.
+  ROCm is needed at *run* time (see below).
+- **ROCm's bundled LLVM (`/opt/rocm/llvm`) cannot be used to build Halide.**
+  It does not ship the `WebAssembly` target that Halide requires, and its
+  version does not track upstream LLVM releases. Use an upstream LLVM.
+- The Makefile build mirrors the NVPTX handling: `WITH_AMDGPU` is set when
+  `llvm-config --targets-built` lists `AMDGPU`.
+
+## HIP Runtime Environment
+
+Halide has no link-time dependency on ROCm. The Halide runtime loads the HIP
+runtime library dynamically the first time a HIP pipeline runs, looking for
+(in order):
+
+- Linux: `libamdhip64.so.7`, `libamdhip64.so.6`, `libamdhip64.so`
+- Windows: `amdhip64_7.dll`, `amdhip64_6.dll`, `amdhip64.dll`
+
+`HL_HIP_LIB=<path or soname>` overrides the search list. If the library cannot
+be loaded, pipelines fail with a device error that names the libraries that
+were tried.
+
+The runtime uses only the HIP *module* API (`hipModuleLoadData`,
+`hipModuleGetFunction`, `hipModuleLaunchKernel`) plus memory/stream calls, all
+resolved by symbol name, so any ROCm from the 5.x through 7.x series that
+provides `libamdhip64` should work. Newer or older versions have not been
+tested. Functions that only exist in newer ROCm releases (`hipDrvMemcpy3D`,
+`hipGetDevicePropertiesR0600`, `hipPointerGetAttributes`, ...) are optional and
+the runtime falls back when they are missing.
+
+Any AMD GPU supported by your ROCm installation can be used, as long as Halide
+compiled the kernels for that GPU's architecture (see the next section). The
+kernels are generated as code-object-version-5 (COV5) HSA code objects by
+default, which ROCm 5.x-7.x accept; see `HL_HIP_CODE_OBJECT_VERSION` below.
+
+Windows (`amdhip64.dll` from the AMD HIP SDK) is supported by the code but has
+not been tested at all.
+
+### Testing your HIP environment
+
+`rocminfo` (from ROCm) lists the agents and their `gfx` names; `rocm-smi`
+shows the devices. A quick way to check that `libamdhip64` is loadable is:
+
+```
+% python3 -c "import ctypes; print(ctypes.CDLL('libamdhip64.so'))"
+```
+
+## Targeting HIP
+
+Add `hip` plus **one** architecture feature to the target:
+
+| Target Feature | Description                                                  |
+| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
+| `hip`          | Enables the HIP backend                                      |
+| `hip_gfx90a`   | AMD Instinct MI200 series (CDNA2), wave64                    |
+| `hip_gfx942`   | AMD Instinct MI300A / MI300X (CDNA3), wave64                 |
+| `hip_gfx950`   | AMD Instinct MI350 series (CDNA4), wave64                    |
+| `hip_gfx1030`  | Radeon RX 6000 (RDNA2), wave32                               |
+| `hip_gfx1100`  | Radeon RX 7900 / W7900 (RDNA3), wave32                       |
+| `hip_gfx1151`  | Ryzen AI Max ("Strix Halo") integrated GPU (RDNA3.5), wave32 |
+| `hip_gfx1201`  | Radeon RX 9070 / R9700 (RDNA4), wave32                       |
+
+The `hip_gfx*` feature selects the LLVM `-mcpu` value. Only one may be set on a
+target (a HIP code object is compiled for a single architecture).
+
+Architectures that have no feature can be selected with the environment
+variable `HL_HIP_ARCH=<gfxNNNN[:xnack-][:sramecc+]>`, which takes precedence
+over any `hip_gfx*` feature (this is the escape hatch for the long tail of AMD
+parts: `gfx908`, `gfx1032`, `gfx1150`, `gfx1250`, ...).
+
+Unlike the CUDA backend, where PTX is forward-compatible and a missing
+`cuda_capability_*` is tolerated, **an AMD code object only runs on the exact
+architecture it was compiled for**. Therefore:
+
+- **AOT**: a target with `hip` but no `hip_gfx*` feature and no `HL_HIP_ARCH`
+  is an error (`hip target requires a hip_gfx* feature or HL_HIP_ARCH`). Be
+  explicit, e.g. `target=host-hip-hip_gfx942`.
+- **JIT**: `HL_JIT_TARGET=host-hip` autodetects the architecture of the GPU in
+  the machine (through `halide_hip_compute_capability`, which reports the
+  device's `gcnArchName`). If the detected architecture has no `hip_gfx*`
+  feature you will get a warning telling you to set `HL_HIP_ARCH`.
+
+Wave (warp) size follows the architecture: gfx9 parts run 64 lanes per wave,
+gfx10 and newer are compiled in wave32 mode. This limits `gpu_lanes()`: the
+lane loop extent must be at most **64 on gfx9 (`hip_gfx90a`, `hip_gfx942`,
+`hip_gfx950`)** and at most **32 on gfx10+ (`hip_gfx1030`, `hip_gfx1100`,
+`hip_gfx1151`, `hip_gfx1201`)**.
+
+For AOT generators, add the features to the target command line option:
+
+```
+$ ./lesson_15_generate -g my_first_generator -o . target=host-hip-hip_gfx942
+```
+
+For JIT apps use the `HL_JIT_TARGET` environment variable:
+
+```
+$ HL_JIT_TARGET=host-hip-hip_gfx1100 ./tutorial/lesson_01_basics
+$ HL_JIT_TARGET=host-hip ./tutorial/lesson_01_basics      # autodetect arch
+```
+
+The debug runtime (`-debug`) prints every HIP call, the kernel launch
+geometry and per-launch timings, exactly like the CUDA debug runtime.
+
+## Environment Variables
+
+Compile-time (read while Halide generates code):
+
+| Variable                     | Effect                                                                                                                                                                     |
+| ---------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `HL_HIP_ARCH`                | `gfxNNNN[:feature...]` — architecture (and target features such as `xnack-`, `sramecc+`) to compile for. Overrides any `hip_gfx*` feature.                                 |
+| `HL_HIP_MATTRS`              | Extra comma-separated LLVM target attributes appended to the ones Halide derives (`+wavefrontsize32` on gfx10+).                                                            |
+| `HL_HIP_CODE_OBJECT_VERSION` | `4`, `5` (default) or `6`. Selects the AMDHSA code object version. `6` is required for `gfx*-generic` targets and needs ROCm 6.3+.                                          |
+| `HL_HIP_USE_EXTERNAL_LLD`    | `1` — link the device object with an external `ld.lld` process instead of the in-process lld library (diagnostic fallback).                                                 |
+| `HL_HIP_LLD`                 | Path to the `ld.lld` executable to use when `HL_HIP_USE_EXTERNAL_LLD=1` (or when Halide was built without the lld libraries). Default: `ld.lld` on `PATH`.                 |
+| `HL_HIP_DUMP_OBJ`            | Directory in which to write every generated device object (`<kernel-module>.o`, the pre-link ET_REL object) and code object (`<kernel-module>.hsaco`). Useful for inspection. |
+| `HL_JIT_TARGET`              | Standard Halide JIT target string, e.g. `host-hip-hip_gfx942` or `host-hip`.                                                                                                |
+| `HL_DEBUG_CODEGEN=2`         | Standard Halide codegen tracing; level 2 prints the device LLVM IR before optimization.                                                                                     |
+
+Run-time (read by the Halide runtime in the generated code):
+
+| Variable                  | Effect                                                                                                                                                   |
+| ------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `HL_HIP_LIB`              | Path or soname of the HIP runtime library to load instead of the built-in `libamdhip64.so.7 / .6 / .so` (`amdhip64*.dll` on Windows) search list.        |
+| `HL_HIP_PACK_KERNARGS`    | `1` — pass kernel arguments through the `extra` / `HIP_LAUNCH_PARAM_BUFFER_POINTER` path with a buffer Halide packs itself, instead of `kernelParams`. Diagnostic. |
+| `HL_HIP_DISABLE_MEMCPY3D` | `1` — never use `hipDrvMemcpy3D` for strided copies; fall back to the recursive 1-D copy loop. Diagnostic.                                              |
+| `HL_GPU_DEVICE`           | Standard Halide device ordinal selection (`halide_get_gpu_device`). Default: the device with the most compute units.                                     |
+
+## Testing
+
+The HIP backend is exercised by the existing GPU correctness tests; no
+HIP-specific test binaries are needed. On a machine with an AMD GPU and ROCm:
+
+```
+$ cmake --build build            # library + tests
+$ HL_JIT_TARGET=host-hip-hip_gfx942 ctest --test-dir build -L correctness \
+      -R "hello_gpu|^correctness_gpu_|device_|async_|atomics|float16|vector_reductions" \
+      --output-on-failure -j 4
+```
+
+Replace `gfx942` with your architecture, or use `HL_JIT_TARGET=host-hip` to
+autodetect it. The AOT generator tests run with `-DHalide_TARGET=host-hip-hip_gfxNNNN`
+at configure time (or `HL_TARGET` in the environment).
+
+Without hardware, the backend can still be validated at the code-object level:
+
+- `HL_TARGET=host-hip-hip_gfx942 HL_HIP_DUMP_OBJ=/tmp/hsaco ./my_generator -g my_gen -o /tmp/out`
+  compiles the pipeline AOT (no ROCm needed) and writes the code objects.
+- `llvm-readelf --file-header --notes /tmp/hsaco/*.hsaco` should show
+  `Machine: EM_AMDGPU`, `OS/ABI: AMDGPU - HSA`, `Type: DYN`, the architecture in
+  the `Flags` line, and an `NT_AMDGPU_METADATA` note describing each kernel's
+  arguments (`.args`), `.kernarg_segment_size`, `.group_segment_fixed_size` and
+  `.wavefront_size`.
+- `llvm-objdump -d --mcpu=gfx942 /tmp/hsaco/*.hsaco` disassembles the kernels
+  (`ds_read`/`ds_write` or `ds_load`/`ds_store` on gfx12 for shared-memory
+  stages, `s_barrier` for `gpu_thread_barrier`).
+- `llvm-readelf -r` should report no relocations.
+
+Halide's internal test for `CodeGen_AMDGPU_Dev` performs these checks
+programmatically for `hip_gfx942` and `hip_gfx1100`.
+
+## Current Status
+
+Phase 1 implements:
+
+- Target features `hip`, `hip_gfx90a`, `hip_gfx942`, `hip_gfx950`,
+  `hip_gfx1030`, `hip_gfx1100`, `hip_gfx1151`, `hip_gfx1201`;
+  `DeviceAPI::HIP`; JIT host-architecture autodetection; `HL_HIP_ARCH`.
+- Device code generation (`src/CodeGen_AMDGPU_Dev.cpp`): `amdgpu_kernel`
+  calling convention, work-item / work-group id intrinsics, fenced
+  `s_barrier`, dynamic LDS for `MemoryType::GPUShared`, private allocations,
+  `f16`/`f32`/`f64` math through LLVM intrinsics (`src/runtime/amdgpu_dev.ll`),
+  integer and floating-point atomics, warp shuffles for `gpu_lanes()` (wave32
+  and wave64), traps for in-kernel assertions, COV5 code objects linked to
+  `ET_DYN` with lld in-process.
+- Runtime (`src/runtime/hip.cpp`, `HalideRuntimeHIP.h`): dynamic loading of
+  `libamdhip64`, module cache, `halide_hip_run`, device malloc with free-list
+  reuse, strided copies (`hipDrvMemcpy3D` when available), crops/slices,
+  wrap/detach of external device pointers, user context hooks
+  (`halide_set_hip_acquire_context` etc.), debug runtime, Windows variant.
+
+Verified so far (2026-09): the library builds with LLVM 22.1.8; generated code
+objects for gfx942 / gfx1100 / gfx1151 / gfx1201 pass the structural checks
+above (see `CodeGen_AMDGPU_Dev` internal test and the compile-verify results
+that accompany the patch series).
+
+**Not yet verified: anything that launches a kernel.** The kernel argument
+ABI, LDS addressing, barriers, atomics, shuffles, copies, allocation reuse,
+object lifetimes and in-kernel asserts have been implemented following the HIP
+and AMDGPU ABI documentation, but no test has been executed on an AMD GPU yet.
+Treat every correctness and performance statement as pending until the
+hardware test results are published.
+
+## Known Limitations / TODO
+
+- **Math library.** Phase 1 maps `sqrt`, `floor`, `ceil`, `round`, `trunc`,
+  `abs`, `fma`, `exp2`, `log2`, `min`, `max`, fast inverse / inverse sqrt to
+  LLVM intrinsics. Transcendentals (`sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `asin`, `exp`, `log`,
+  `pow`, `erf`, ...) fall back to Halide's generic implementations; phase 2
+  vendors ROCm's `ocml` device library (Apache-2.0 with LLVM exception, like
+  `libdevice` for CUDA) for accuracy and speed parity with the CUDA backend.
+- **Single architecture per target.** Phase 2 will allow several `hip_gfx*`
+  features and emit a clang offload bundle (one code object per architecture)
+  which `hipModuleLoadData` selects from at run time.
+- **Windows** builds `windows_hip.cpp` but has never been run.
+- **No tensor-core / matrix (MFMA, WMMA) paths** — Halide has no scheduling
+  primitive for them on any GPU backend.
+- **No cooperative groups, no HIP graphs, no multi-stream overlap** beyond what
+  Halide's `async()` / device-copy machinery provides.
+- Fine-grained (host-coherent) memory is not used; all device allocations are
+  `hipMalloc` (coarse-grained), which is what hardware floating-point atomics
+  require.
+- `gfx*-generic` targets require `HL_HIP_CODE_OBJECT_VERSION=6` and ROCm 6.3+;
+  not tested.
+- Performance has not been tuned at all.
+
+## Design Notes
+
+- **Shared memory.** Halide's other GPU backends use a null pointer in the
+  shared address space as the base of the dynamically sized shared allocation.
+  On AMDGPU the LDS null pointer is `0xFFFFFFFF`, so that idiom would fault.
+  Instead the backend declares
+  `@__halide_dynamic_lds = external addrspace(3) global [0 x i8], align 16`
+  and binds every `MemoryType::GPUShared` allocation to it; the actual size is
+  passed as `sharedMemBytes` to `hipModuleLaunchKernel`, and the kernel's
+  `.group_segment_fixed_size` stays 0.
+- **Kernel arguments.** Halide passes `kernelParams` (an array of pointers to
+  each argument) to `hipModuleLaunchKernel`; the HIP runtime packs the kernarg
+  segment using the `.args` offsets from the code object's metadata and appends
+  the hidden arguments itself. Halide never lays out the kernarg buffer by hand
+  (`HL_HIP_PACK_KERNARGS=1` is a diagnostic path that does).
+- **Linking.** The AMDGPU backend produces a relocatable object; HSA requires a
+  linked shared object. Halide links it in-process with lld's ELF driver using
+  the same command line clang uses for HIP device links
+  (`ld.lld -flavor gnu -m elf64_amdgpu --no-undefined -shared`), under a global
+  mutex. This is deliberately different from the 2018 attempt at an AMDGPU
+  backend, which patched relocations by hand and never reached a loadable
+  object.
+- **Code object version.** COV5 (`amdhsa_code_object_version` module flag =
+  500) is the default because it is accepted by every ROCm from 5.x to 7.x.
+  LLVM's own default is COV6.
+- **Buffer pointers** are kernel arguments in `addrspace(1)` (global) with
+  `noalias`; they are cast to flat pointers on entry so that the code inherited
+  from `CodeGen_LLVM` works unchanged, and LLVM's `InferAddressSpaces` recovers
+  global loads/stores.
+- **Barriers** are `fence syncscope("workgroup") release; s_barrier; fence
+  syncscope("workgroup") acquire` — `s_barrier` alone is not a memory fence on
+  AMDGPU.
+- **Denormals** are flushed (`denormal-fp-math-f32=preserve-sign`) unless the
+  target has `strict_float`, matching the CUDA backend's `ftz` behaviour.
